 "title": "WHAT AN EXPERIENCE CAN OWN",
 "date": "2026-11-18",
 "question": "Store a repair in its most invariant form -- a shape of site, a demanded value, an expression -- and does it reach a program nobody experienced, and how far does that go?",
 "what_the_two_transfers_are": {
  "longest_common_subsequence": "the stored template (__S0__)[(1):] applied at the argument b of the recursive call gives longest_common_subsequence(a, b[1:]) -- the published fix verbatim",
  "next_palindrome": "the same template applied at digit_list gives len(digit_list[1:]) where the published fix is len(digit_list) - 1 -- the same function of the same value, a different spelling",
  "checked_by_hand": true
 },
 "the_tradeoff_it_measures": "Generality and information are one axis. The pair that carries the fix (get_factors) matches 0 unseen sites; the pair that carries almost nothing (a bare name indexed from 1) matches 104 and fixes 2 (1.92%). No representation of an experience escapes that axis; what escapes it is asking at the site, which is exact.",
 "what_the_curve_says": "Addressability of unseen sites rises 34.4% (1 experience) -> 59.7% (24 experiences) and flattens: the marginal experience is worth about one point. Experience buys a saturating share of the surface, never the surface.",
 "the_key": "a builtin is an operation and stays literal (len, max, sorted); a name a program defines is data and becomes a positional slot (__S0__, __S1__)",
 "the_frontier_this_is_compared_against": {
  "SWE-bench Verified": "state of the art ~87.6% (Claude Opus 4.7, Apr 2026, vendor); frontier agents exceed 75%",
  "SWE-bench ProMax": "best 41.2% (GPT-5.2), tasks average 11.4 files and 261.6 lines",
  "long horizon": "all state-of-the-art agents below 20% on long-horizon tasks",
